full_judgment:
Petitioner by a registered deed of Trust dated 3-12-79 created a Trust for welfare and charitable purposes. The trustees of the Trust decided to undertake a Housing Estate Scheme for the purpose of utilizing the income therefrom for the welfare and charitable purposes of the Trust. In the month of January, 1980, the petitioner approached the then Minister-in-Charge of the Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms for allotment of Government Khas land for the said Trust. Petitioner on 16-1-80 submitted an application for settlement of Government khas land to the said Minister who on the body of the application passed on order for submission of enquiry report. Respondent No. 4, the Additional Deputy Commissioner (Revenue), Sylhet on 13-2-1980 submitted report. The Minister on consideration of the report passed an order for settlement of 31 acres of khas land of plot No. 1 of Mouza Debpur, PS Kotwali, Dist. Sylhet in favour of the OJ Trust at a salami of Taka 1,50,000.00. Respondent No. 4, Additional Deputy Commissioner (Revenue), in obedience to the said order of the Minister, allotted the said land in favour of OJ Trust, which paid the salami and Government Tahsilder delivered possession of the said land on 20-2-1980 to the Trust and, thereafter, on 22-2-1980 respondent No. 4, executed and registered lease deed in favour of OJ Trust.&nbsp; 

 3. It is the further case of the petitioner that the Superintendent of the Government Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm put forward claim on the said land and approached the Government for not allowing the said Trust to utilize the land. It transpired, subsequently, that 228 acres of land including the disputed land were acquired for Cattle Breeding- cum-Dairy Farm in the year 1932 in Land Acquisition Case Nos. 220-240 of 1930-31. The said land was not recorded in the name of the said Farm but recorded as Government khas land. The Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms requested the Ministry of Agriculture, Fisheries and Livestock&rsquo;s to give up the claim over the said land and the Ministry of Fishery and Livestock had been pleased to agree to surrender the said 21 acres of land in favour of OJ Trust and the said decision was communicated by a Memo dated 5th of January, 1986 (Annexure-&#39;&#39;H&#39;&#39;). Subsequently, the Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms by a Memo dated 21-5-86 (Annexure-T) cancelled the allotment granted in favour of OJ Trust and asked the Deputy Commissioner, Sylhet to revoke the deed of lease and find out khas land elsewhere for the Trust. The petitioner&#39;&#39;s prayer dated 31-5-86 addressed to the Government for revision of the said decision stood rejected and the petitioner was asked to submit proposal for lease of alternative Government khas land. Thereafter, the said Ministry revoked the earlier decision dated 21-5-86 and the Deputy Land Reforms Commissioner by a Memo dated 24-12-88 (Annexure-&#39;&#39;K&#39;&#39;) conveyed the decision of the Ministry to the Deputy Commissioner, Sylhet.&nbsp; 

 7. In the Affidavit-in-opposition filed on behalf of the respondent No. 1, it is stated that the said 31 acres of land of plot No. 1 of Mouza Debpur, PS Kotwali, District, Sylhet was acquired in Land Acquisition Case No. 235 of 1930-31 for Government Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm, Sylhet and the said Farm has been in possession of the said land since 1932. The said land was recorded as khas land by mistake in the khas khatian. The Khatian illegally prepared in the name of OJ Trust with respect to the land in question was cancelled by the Revenue Authority on 6-1-87. It is further stated and asserted that the Ministry of Land had no interest in the land in question as the same was acquired for Government Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm. The Government in Memo No. V-239/84/559 dated 23-8-84 addressed to the Deputy Commissioner, Sylhet categorically stated that the land in question belonged to Government Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm, Sylhet and the land was wrongly leased out to OJ Trust and asked the Deputy Commissioner to do the needful.&nbsp; 

 16. The admitted position is that 228 acres of land had been acquired in LA Case Nos. 220-240 of 1930-31 including 31 acres of land in question for Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm at Sylhet. The said Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm is admittedly under the control and management of the respondent No. 3, Ministry of Fisheries and Livestock of the Government of Bangladesh. It is not denied by the petitioner that the said 228 acres of land including 31 acres of land in question had not been acquired for Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm at Sylhet. It is the positive common case set up by both the respondent Nos. 1 and 3 that Sylhet Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm had been and has been in possession of the entire acquired 228 acres of land including 31 acres of land in question since the year 1932. The respondent No. 1, the Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms in the affidavit-in-opposition categorically stated that the land in question belonged to Sylhet Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm which is under the control of the respondent No. 3&#39;&#39;s Ministry of Fisheries and Livestock. In the backdrop of this categorical assertion made by the respondent No. 1 as to the respondent No. 3&#39;&#39;s control over the land in question, the point became manifestly clear that it was only respondent No. 3&#39;&#39;s Ministry who had the exclusive jurisdiction and competence to lease out the land in possession of the Sylhet Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm. The respondent No. 1&#39;&#39;s Ministry did not have any authority, whatsoever, to lease out the disputed land in favour of the petitioner OJ Trust, merely because, they said land was not recorded in the name of the Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm but wrongly recorded in the khatian as Government khas land.&nbsp; 

 17. Perused the writ petition and the documents annexed thereto, the two affidavits-in-opposition filed by respondent Nos. 1 and 3 and the documents annexed thereto. We also perused the relevant Government files produced before us by the learned Deputy Attorney-General. On consideration of all these papers and documents we find that the process leading to the execution and registration of the lease deed had been done in a hot haste. OJ Trust was created on 3-12-79 by a registered deed of Trust with the sum of Taka one lac as its fund. Annexure-B is the deed of Trust. The Trust was created for the purpose of performing various charitable purposes, such as relief of the people in general and specially for the poor in regard to their education, health, medical treatment and for general cultural and social activities and other amenities of life and also for various research and scientific projects and religious and charitable purposes. The deed of Trust authorizes the trustees to invest the Trust Fund in any business, industry, security, etc. as the trustees may decide. In the month of January, 1980, petitioner met the Hon&#39;&#39;ble Minister-in-charge of the Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms for allotment of khas land in favour of the said Trust. An application had been filed before the said Minster on 16-1-80. Annexure-C is the said application. We perused the said application. In the application, nowhere it was stated, that the income of the Housing Estate would be utilized for the purposes of the Trust. The prayer for allocation of Government khas land at Sylhet was for a Housing Estate Scheme for the expatriation living in Britain. The Hon&#39;&#39;ble Minister on the body of the application passed an order in the following words: &quot;ADC (Rev.), Sylhet will please enquire and report positively within 2 weeks whether the lands are vacant khas lands and could be leased out to the petitioner trust on long term basis. To 7-2-80 for orders.&quot; Enquiry report was submitted on 13-2-80. The Hon&#39;&#39;ble Minister on 16-2-80 passed an order for settlement of the land in favour of petitioner OJ Trust at a salami of Taka 1,50,000.00. The possession of the allotted land was allegedly given to the petitioner on 20-2-80. Deed of lease was executed and registered on 22-2-80. Annexure-F is the Photostat copy of the certified copy of the deed of lease. In the said deed of lease, there is no mention at all that the income of the Housing Estate would be utilized to materialize the purposes of the Trust. In the deed of lease there is no recital that the possession had been handed over to the lessee OJ Trust though the possession was allegedly delivered to the lessee on 20-2-1980. The deed of lease was executed within a period of two months and sixteen days of the creation of Trust Deed.&nbsp; 

 18. Annexure-D to the affidavit-in-opposition filed on behalf of the respondent No. 3 is Memo dated 12-4-1980 sent by the then Sub-Divisional Officer, Kotwali, Sylhet addressed to the Deputy Commissioner, Sylhet. That Memo is nothing but an Enquiry Report. In the said Report, the Sub-Divisional Officer stated that he personally visited the spot and found that the land in question was part and parcel of the acquired land and the land is well demarcated by barbed wire fencing with RCC pillars which appeared to be as old as the age of the acquisition. The Sub-Divisional Officer further reported that many attempts were made for getting settlement of the said land and in all the Enquiry Reports submitted by the Circle Officers, (Revenue), Kotwali it was stated that the land in question was in possession of the Dairy Farm and the area is covered by RCC posts and barbed wire fencing and being used for grazing purpose. It was further reported that the Circle Officer (Revenue), Kotwali by suppressing facts reported that the land in question was khas land and the same could be settled in favour of Obaid Jaigirdar.&nbsp; 

 19. Annexure-A to the affidavit-in-opposition of the respondent No. 3 is a letter dated 7-5-1980 addressed to the Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms by the Deputy Commissioner, Sylhet. In the said letter, the aforesaid Enquiry Report of the Sub-Divisional Officer had been referred to. The Deputy Commissioner opined that the land in question was in possession of the Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm. In the said letter the Deputy Commissioner stated that he for the first time came to know of the illegal settlement matter in favour of OJ Trust from the Superintendent of the Farm when he approached him and intimated of the alleged settlement. Thereafter, the respondent No. 3&#39;&#39;s Ministry made a series of letters and correspondences with the respondent No. 1&#39;&#39;s Ministry for cancelling the lease granted in favour of OJ Trust. It appears to us that in the backdrop of the letters and correspondences and the actual state of affairs, the Memo dated 21-5-1986 had been issued. Annexure-C to the writ petition is the Memo dated 21-5-1986 by which the respondent No. 1&#39;&#39;s Ministry cancelled the allotment in favour of petitioner OJ Trust with the decision that the Dairy Farm was a very important Government Project and no land of the Farm should be given to an individual on the plea that the same was recorded in collector&#39;&#39;s khatian as khas land and the Government Dairy Farm could not be penalised for wrong recording of the land as khas land. The Ministry in the said Memo asked the Deputy Commissioner to take appropriate step to revoke the lease deeds and to find out land elsewhere for settlement to OJ Trust and send proposal to the Ministry for consideration with copy to the petitioner. But neither the Deputy Commissioner nor the petitioner appears to have taken any further step in the matter.&nbsp; 

 21. From plethora of letters and correspondences annexed as annexures and also letters and correspondences from the Government file, it becomes evidently clear that the respondent No. 3&#39;&#39;s Ministry had been pursuing the respondent No. 1&#39;&#39;s Ministry for cancelling the illegal lease granted to OJ Trust. From all these annexure and letters, we find that the entire acquired 228 acres of land including the land in question since the year 1932 has been in possession of Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm and the land was illegally leased out to OJ Trust on the basis of the Enquiry Report submitted by the Circle Officer (Revenue), Kotwali, Syihet falsely showing the land in question as Government khas land by suppressing the fact that the same was in possession of the Dairy Farm. Thus, lease in favour of the petitioner OJ Trust was the result of fraudulent misrepresentation of facts to the respondent No. 1&#39;&#39;s Ministry.&nbsp; 

 31. The facts and circumstances of the cited case is quite distinguishable and got no manner of application in the fact and circumstances of the present case. In our view, the said decision cannot be of any assistance to the petitioner who is not an innocent party in the transaction. Petitioner can take advantage of respondent No. 1&#39;&#39;s offer of lease of Government khas land elsewhere but he cannot insist on asserting title on the land of respondent No. 3&nbsp;
